  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Randy V\u00e1squez didn't give them much to work with, and they didn't need it.",
    "what_this_means": "Control was the whole game. V\u00e1squez moved through Pittsburgh's lineup on five innings, one hit, nothing scored. The bullpen took the rest. Hart, Canning, Matsui, Rodriguez chaining 3.3 scoreless innings without drama. Two runs scored off Pirates pitching before anyone was really looking, then the Padres held the line for the final five innings. Jackson Merrill drove in one early. Luis Rengifo added another. That was the offense. Seven hits, two runs, enough to win a game decided by starvation, not struggle. Nothing flashy. Nothing that asked the bats to carry weight they couldn't lift. The starter and the back end made those two runs permanent.",
    "what_to_watch": "This is how this team actually wins. Not on breakouts or late rallies, but on structure so tight it strangles opponents into submission. The pitching set the terms. One hit in five innings is not a negotiation. And the offense didn't need to answer a second time. Merrill and Rengifo converted their moments. The rest of the lineup did what it was supposed to do: stay out of the way and trust the formula. This is fragile, because it always is. Two runs is not a margin that survives carelessness. But today it survived, and the gap to first tightened by one. They won by doing exactly what they've learned to do when the staff is locked in. Pitching doesn't lose games like this.",
